{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts uses a small grammar so an agent can reason from data and\nintent instead of selecting a monolithic chart component. The safest authoring\npath is explicit and repeatable."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-authoring-sequence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The authoring sequence"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State the analytical question in one sentence."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify each field's semantic type: quantitative, temporal, ordinal, or\nidentifier."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the smallest mark composition that answers the question."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose compact scales for common positional and categorical color channels."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decide which preparation belongs in application code, D3, SQL, or a server."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add accessible labeling and the default focus/tooltip behavior."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verify a static scene before adding animation or interaction."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add custom behavior only at a documented extension boundary."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start at "},{"type":"link","href":"./choosing-a-chart.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choosing a Chart"}]},{"type":"text","value":", then use the relevant\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"example family"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put Cartesian scale and axis options under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales.x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales.y"},{"type":"text","value":". The\npre-Alpha root "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" form is not part of the Alpha API. Inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polar()"},{"type":"text","value":",\nput position scales under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales.angle"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales.radius"},{"type":"text","value":". Use named registry\nentries and mark-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angleScale"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radiusScale"},{"type":"text","value":"\nselectors when a chart needs more than one mapping on the same channel."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep the default SVG renderer unless a measured paint-heavy mark benefits from\nCanvas. To mix surfaces, import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"canvasChartRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" from the exact\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/canvas"},{"type":"text","value":" subpath and set that mark's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderer"},{"type":"text","value":" option. Do not\nmove the complete chart to Canvas when only one dense mark needs raster paint."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"canonical-sources","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Canonical sources"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use one documentation owner for each decision:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"grammar and channel semantics:\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/grammar-of-graphics.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grammar of Graphics"}]},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"compact scales and D3 upgrade ownership:\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"responsive layout:\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./responsive-charts.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"focus and tooltip behavior:\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"large-data representation:\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./large-data.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large Data"}]},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"exact signatures and options:\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"API Reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not reconstruct an API from an example when the reference owns the\nsignature. Do not restate D3 behavior when the D3 bridge links to its\nauthoritative documentation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-smallest-scale","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the smallest scale"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use this order before adding "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Semantic need"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First choice"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric two-stop axis"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/linear"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Categorical intervals such as bars"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/band"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Categorical positions such as dots"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/point"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Categorical colors"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Time, UTC, nonlinear, piecewise, or continuous color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Granular "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" import"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the compact factory itself when the rendered channels should determine\nthe domain. Return a configured scale from a zero-argument factory when it\nneeds padding or another option before inference. Pass an instance when the\ndomain is application state. Never assign a positional pixel range; Charts\nowns the responsive range."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upgrade only the scale whose semantics exceed the compact subset. For example,\na continuous UTC x axis may use D3 while a numeric y axis remains compact.\nUsing one D3 scale does not require replacing compact band, point, linear, or\nordinal scales elsewhere in the chart."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"prefer-complete-typed-examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer complete, typed examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generated code should include:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every import and its exact subpath;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the datum and captured application-value interfaces;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"scale construction;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a complete chart definition;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"complete adapter or host usage;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a meaningful "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ariaLabel"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stable inferred or explicit identity;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"empty and constant-domain policies when applicable."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a compact chart, import exact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/*"},{"type":"text","value":" entries and do not\nadd "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@types/d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":". If an advanced scale requires D3, declare\nthe granular runtime and matching type package as direct application\ndependencies and state which missing compact capability required the upgrade."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It should not require readers to invent undeclared variables, hidden imports,\ncasts, or CSS needed for correctness."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"use-the-public-boundary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the public boundary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Allowed building blocks are public package exports and documented external\nmodules. Never import a private source file because it appears convenient in\nthe repository."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the requested result cannot be expressed:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Try built-in mark composition."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a public custom mark."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a custom focus strategy, spatial index, or SVG renderer when that is the\nactual missing boundary."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reduce any remaining gap to a small failing example and record the API\nfriction."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not hide a missing capability behind "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"any"},{"type":"text","value":", double casts, or manual DOM\nmutation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"validate-generated-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validate generated work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run, in order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type checking with no unexpected suppression."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A deterministic scene assertion."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A browser interaction test when the chart is interactive."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Light and dark visual checks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A narrow production bundle measurement when a new capability is imported."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For compact-scale work, verify that the bundle retains the selected family but\nnot "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-format"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-interpolate"},{"type":"text","value":", or sibling compact families."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For changing charts, also test reorder, resize, empty data, replacement data,\nand a burst that must settle on the latest definition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"request-template","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request template"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use this structure when asking an agent to build a chart:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"Question:\nData shape and semantic field types:\nRequired encodings:\nInteraction and selection:\nResponsive container:\nAccessibility summary:\nExpected update behavior:\nBundle constraints:\nAcceptance checks:","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When information is missing, choose documented defaults for presentation.\nAsk before inventing analytical semantics, aggregations, or selection behavior\nthat would change the meaning of the data."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"skills-boundary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skills boundary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data exploration, chart recommendation, anomaly investigation, and iterative\nanalysis belong in agent skills. The runtime remains a small deterministic\nrendering library."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skills should route back to these canonical pages and official data-tool\ndocumentation. They should add problem-solving procedures and validation, not\ncopy the API reference into another source of truth."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"the-authoring-sequence","text":"The authoring sequence","level":2},{"id":"canonical-sources","text":"Canonical sources","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-smallest-scale","text":"Choose the smallest scale","level":2},{"id":"prefer-complete-typed-examples","text":"Prefer complete, typed examples","level":2},{"id":"use-the-public-boundary","text":"Use the public boundary","level":2},{"id":"validate-generated-work","text":"Validate generated work","level":2},{"id":"request-template","text":"Request template","level":2},{"id":"skills-boundary","text":"Skills boundary","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Charts uses a small grammar so an agent can reason from data and\nintent instead of selecting a monolithic chart component. The safest authoring\npath is explicit and repeatable.\n\n## The authoring sequence\n\n1. State the analytical question in one sentence.\n2. Identify each field's semantic type: quantitative, temporal, ordinal, or\n   identifier.\n3. Choose the smallest mark composition that answers the question.\n4. Choose compact scales for common positional and categorical color channels.\n5. Decide which preparation belongs in application code, D3, SQL, or a server.\n6. Add accessible labeling and the default focus/tooltip behavior.\n7. Verify a static scene before adding animation or interaction.\n8. Add custom behavior only at a documented extension boundary.\n\nStart at [Choosing a Chart](./choosing-a-chart.md), then use the relevant\n[example family](../examples/index.md).\n\nPut Cartesian scale and axis options under `scales.x` and `scales.y`. The\npre-Alpha root `x` and `y` form is not part of the Alpha API. Inside `polar()`,\nput position scales under `scales.angle` and `scales.radius`. Use named registry\nentries and mark-level `xScale`, `yScale`, `angleScale`, or `radiusScale`\nselectors when a chart needs more than one mapping on the same channel.\n\nKeep the default SVG renderer unless a measured paint-heavy mark benefits from\nCanvas. To mix surfaces, import `canvasChartRenderer` from the exact\n`@tanstack/charts/canvas` subpath and set that mark's `renderer` option. Do not\nmove the complete chart to Canvas when only one dense mark needs raster paint.\n\n## Canonical sources\n\nUse one documentation owner for each decision:\n\n- grammar and channel semantics:\n  [Grammar of Graphics](../concepts/grammar-of-graphics.md);\n- compact scales and D3 upgrade ownership:\n  [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md);\n- responsive layout:\n  [Responsive Charts](./responsive-charts.md);\n- focus and tooltip behavior:\n  [Tooltips and Focus](./tooltips-and-focus.md);\n- large-data representation:\n  [Large Data](./large-data.md);\n- exact signatures and options:\n  [API Reference](../reference/index.md).\n\nDo not reconstruct an API from an example when the reference owns the\nsignature. Do not restate D3 behavior when the D3 bridge links to its\nauthoritative documentation.\n\n## Choose the smallest scale\n\nUse this order before adding `d3-scale`:\n\n| Semantic need                                        | First choice                      |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Numeric two-stop axis                                | `@tanstack/charts/scales/linear`  |\n| Categorical intervals such as bars                   | `@tanstack/charts/scales/band`    |\n| Categorical positions such as dots                   | `@tanstack/charts/scales/point`   |\n| Categorical colors                                   | `@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal` |\n| Time, UTC, nonlinear, piecewise, or continuous color | Granular `d3-scale` import        |\n\nPass the compact factory itself when the rendered channels should determine\nthe domain. Return a configured scale from a zero-argument factory when it\nneeds padding or another option before inference. Pass an instance when the\ndomain is application state. Never assign a positional pixel range; Charts\nowns the responsive range.\n\nUpgrade only the scale whose semantics exceed the compact subset. For example,\na continuous UTC x axis may use D3 while a numeric y axis remains compact.\nUsing one D3 scale does not require replacing compact band, point, linear, or\nordinal scales elsewhere in the chart.\n\n## Prefer complete, typed examples\n\nGenerated code should include:\n\n- every import and its exact subpath;\n- the datum and captured application-value interfaces;\n- scale construction;\n- a complete chart definition;\n- complete adapter or host usage;\n- a meaningful `ariaLabel`;\n- stable inferred or explicit identity;\n- empty and constant-domain policies when applicable.\n\nFor a compact chart, import exact `@tanstack/charts/scales/*` entries and do not\nadd `d3-scale` or `@types/d3-scale`. If an advanced scale requires D3, declare\nthe granular runtime and matching type package as direct application\ndependencies and state which missing compact capability required the upgrade.\n\nIt should not require readers to invent undeclared variables, hidden imports,\ncasts, or CSS needed for correctness.\n\n## Use the public boundary\n\nAllowed building blocks are public package exports and documented external\nmodules. Never import a private source file because it appears convenient in\nthe repository.\n\nIf the requested result cannot be expressed:\n\n1. Try built-in mark composition.\n2. Use a public custom mark.\n3. Use a custom focus strategy, spatial index, or SVG renderer when that is the\n   actual missing boundary.\n4. Reduce any remaining gap to a small failing example and record the API\n   friction.\n\nDo not hide a missing capability behind `any`, double casts, or manual DOM\nmutation.\n\n## Validate generated work\n\nRun, in order:\n\n1. Type checking with no unexpected suppression.\n2. A deterministic scene assertion.\n3. A browser interaction test when the chart is interactive.\n4. Light and dark visual checks.\n5. A narrow production bundle measurement when a new capability is imported.\n\nFor compact-scale work, verify that the bundle retains the selected family but\nnot `d3-scale`, `d3-format`, `d3-interpolate`, or sibling compact families.\n\nFor changing charts, also test reorder, resize, empty data, replacement data,\nand a burst that must settle on the latest definition.\n\n## Request template\n\nUse this structure when asking an agent to build a chart:\n\n```text\nQuestion:\nData shape and semantic field types:\nRequired encodings:\nInteraction and selection:\nResponsive container:\nAccessibility summary:\nExpected update behavior:\nBundle constraints:\nAcceptance checks:\n```\n\nWhen information is missing, choose documented defaults for presentation.\nAsk before inventing analytical semantics, aggregations, or selection behavior\nthat would change the meaning of the data.\n\n## Skills boundary\n\nData exploration, chart recommendation, anomaly investigation, and iterative\nanalysis belong in agent skills. The runtime remains a small deterministic\nrendering library.\n\nSkills should route back to these canonical pages and official data-tool\ndocumentation. They should add problem-solving procedures and validation, not\ncopy the API reference into another source of truth.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: AI Authoring\ndescription: Give coding agents a deterministic route from a visualization goal to typed TanStack Charts code, validation, and advanced extension.\n---\n\nTanStack Charts uses a small grammar so an agent can reason from data and\nintent instead of selecting a monolithic chart component. The safest authoring\npath is explicit and repeatable.\n\n## The authoring sequence\n\n1. State the analytical question in one sentence.\n2. Identify each field's semantic type: quantitative, temporal, ordinal, or\n   identifier.\n3. Choose the smallest mark composition that answers the question.\n4. Choose compact scales for common positional and categorical color channels.\n5. Decide which preparation belongs in application code, D3, SQL, or a server.\n6. Add accessible labeling and the default focus/tooltip behavior.\n7. Verify a static scene before adding animation or interaction.\n8. Add custom behavior only at a documented extension boundary.\n\nStart at [Choosing a Chart](./choosing-a-chart.md), then use the relevant\n[example family](../examples/index.md).\n\nPut Cartesian scale and axis options under `scales.x` and `scales.y`. The\npre-Alpha root `x` and `y` form is not part of the Alpha API. Inside `polar()`,\nput position scales under `scales.angle` and `scales.radius`. Use named registry\nentries and mark-level `xScale`, `yScale`, `angleScale`, or `radiusScale`\nselectors when a chart needs more than one mapping on the same channel.\n\nKeep the default SVG renderer unless a measured paint-heavy mark benefits from\nCanvas. To mix surfaces, import `canvasChartRenderer` from the exact\n`@tanstack/charts/canvas` subpath and set that mark's `renderer` option. Do not\nmove the complete chart to Canvas when only one dense mark needs raster paint.\n\n## Canonical sources\n\nUse one documentation owner for each decision:\n\n- grammar and channel semantics:\n  [Grammar of Graphics](../concepts/grammar-of-graphics.md);\n- compact scales and D3 upgrade ownership:\n  [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md);\n- responsive layout:\n  [Responsive Charts](./responsive-charts.md);\n- focus and tooltip behavior:\n  [Tooltips and Focus](./tooltips-and-focus.md);\n- large-data representation:\n  [Large Data](./large-data.md);\n- exact signatures and options:\n  [API Reference](../reference/index.md).\n\nDo not reconstruct an API from an example when the reference owns the\nsignature. Do not restate D3 behavior when the D3 bridge links to its\nauthoritative documentation.\n\n## Choose the smallest scale\n\nUse this order before adding `d3-scale`:\n\n| Semantic need                                        | First choice                      |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Numeric two-stop axis                                | `@tanstack/charts/scales/linear`  |\n| Categorical intervals such as bars                   | `@tanstack/charts/scales/band`    |\n| Categorical positions such as dots                   | `@tanstack/charts/scales/point`   |\n| Categorical colors                                   | `@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal` |\n| Time, UTC, nonlinear, piecewise, or continuous color | Granular `d3-scale` import        |\n\nPass the compact factory itself when the rendered channels should determine\nthe domain. Return a configured scale from a zero-argument factory when it\nneeds padding or another option before inference. Pass an instance when the\ndomain is application state. Never assign a positional pixel range; Charts\nowns the responsive range.\n\nUpgrade only the scale whose semantics exceed the compact subset. For example,\na continuous UTC x axis may use D3 while a numeric y axis remains compact.\nUsing one D3 scale does not require replacing compact band, point, linear, or\nordinal scales elsewhere in the chart.\n\n## Prefer complete, typed examples\n\nGenerated code should include:\n\n- every import and its exact subpath;\n- the datum and captured application-value interfaces;\n- scale construction;\n- a complete chart definition;\n- complete adapter or host usage;\n- a meaningful `ariaLabel`;\n- stable inferred or explicit identity;\n- empty and constant-domain policies when applicable.\n\nFor a compact chart, import exact `@tanstack/charts/scales/*` entries and do not\nadd `d3-scale` or `@types/d3-scale`. If an advanced scale requires D3, declare\nthe granular runtime and matching type package as direct application\ndependencies and state which missing compact capability required the upgrade.\n\nIt should not require readers to invent undeclared variables, hidden imports,\ncasts, or CSS needed for correctness.\n\n## Use the public boundary\n\nAllowed building blocks are public package exports and documented external\nmodules. Never import a private source file because it appears convenient in\nthe repository.\n\nIf the requested result cannot be expressed:\n\n1. Try built-in mark composition.\n2. Use a public custom mark.\n3. Use a custom focus strategy, spatial index, or SVG renderer when that is the\n   actual missing boundary.\n4. Reduce any remaining gap to a small failing example and record the API\n   friction.\n\nDo not hide a missing capability behind `any`, double casts, or manual DOM\nmutation.\n\n## Validate generated work\n\nRun, in order:\n\n1. Type checking with no unexpected suppression.\n2. A deterministic scene assertion.\n3. A browser interaction test when the chart is interactive.\n4. Light and dark visual checks.\n5. A narrow production bundle measurement when a new capability is imported.\n\nFor compact-scale work, verify that the bundle retains the selected family but\nnot `d3-scale`, `d3-format`, `d3-interpolate`, or sibling compact families.\n\nFor changing charts, also test reorder, resize, empty data, replacement data,\nand a burst that must settle on the latest definition.\n\n## Request template\n\nUse this structure when asking an agent to build a chart:\n\n```text\nQuestion:\nData shape and semantic field types:\nRequired encodings:\nInteraction and selection:\nResponsive container:\nAccessibility summary:\nExpected update behavior:\nBundle constraints:\nAcceptance checks:\n```\n\nWhen information is missing, choose documented defaults for presentation.\nAsk before inventing analytical semantics, aggregations, or selection behavior\nthat would change the meaning of the data.\n\n## Skills boundary\n\nData exploration, chart recommendation, anomaly investigation, and iterative\nanalysis belong in agent skills. The runtime remains a small deterministic\nrendering library.\n\nSkills should route back to these canonical pages and official data-tool\ndocumentation. They should add problem-solving procedures and validation, not\ncopy the API reference into another source of truth.\n"}
